Represent molecular connectivity with labels and justified equivalences (@chemistry-connectivity: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Count-small-saturated-carbon-skeletons-without-counting-drawings-twice.lingua#r_00E0A2FD34574493511BF8F2AC") { r_007DFC5107CA477D570B05BDDC
A finite molecular graph has a vertex for each represented atom and an edge for each represented bond. Vertex labels can include element, isotope and charge; edge labels can include bond order. Choose them to match the chemical model. A graph with all hydrogens explicit and a heavy-atom skeleton with implicit hydrogens can represent the same proposed molecule only when a stated completion rule connects them. Aromatic or delocalized bonding needs its own representation conventions; a single integer bond-order drawing does not capture the entire electronic state.

Two such graphs have the same represented connectivity if there is a bijection of their vertices preserving all retained labels and edges with their labels. That bijection is an isomorphism. It does not need to preserve arbitrary atom numbers or the positions on a page. The composition of two isomorphisms and the inverse of one are again isomorphisms, so this comparison is an equivalence relation. Reuse [Separate equivalence classes from ordering and reachability](../../mathematics/core/Separate-equivalence-classes-from-ordering-and-reachability.lingua#r_00844E60FB32CCD3D5D2D1B50D) and [Define a graph walk before reasoning about what can happen](../../mathematics/core/Define-a-graph-walk-before-reasoning-about-what-can-happen.lingua#r_00F6592164EEB9B8572CCD8486) for the underlying finite definitions.

For an original carbon-skeleton example, graph A has edges {01,12,23}; graph B has edges {03,32,21}. Mapping vertices (0,1,2,3) to (0,3,2,1) carries A to B. Both are a four-vertex path. Graph C has edges {01,02,03}; it has one vertex of degree three and three of degree one. The path degrees are 1,1,2,2, so no isomorphism can map A to C. Degree is the number of incident edges in this simple single-bond model. A graph isomorphism must preserve degree, making unequal degree sequences a sufficient witness of difference.

Equal degree sequences do not prove equivalence. Consider a six-vertex tree with edges {01,02,03,34,45}, and another with {01,02,23,04,45}. Both have degrees 3,2,2,1,1,1. From their unique degree-three vertex, however, the branch lengths to leaves are respectively {1,1,3} and {1,2,2}. An isomorphism must preserve those distances, so the trees differ. They are the skeleton patterns behind two distinct branched hexanes. A summary descriptor can discard exactly the structural information a later question needs.

A canonical encoding chooses one reproducible description per equivalence class. For a tree rooted at vertex v, encode a leaf by (), and encode a nonleaf by parentheses around the sorted encodings of its child subtrees. Induction on tree size shows that equal rooted codes mean isomorphic rooted trees: the codes identify matching child subtrees, and conversely an isomorphism pairs them. Taking the least code over every possible root yields a complete invariant for these unrooted trees. Equal minimum codes provide an isomorphism through their achieving roots; isomorphic trees have the same root-code collection. This proof depends on acyclicity. Applying the same recursive rule to a cyclic graph would revisit vertices and would not be justified.

**Work:** write the explicit isomorphism for the first two paths, prove the degree obstruction for the star, and reconstruct the six-vertex counterexample using graph distances. Explain why comparing just a hash of a code would add a possible collision unless equality of the original codes is checked. The study retains full codes. Bond graphs are useful for identity reasoning; valence compatibility and graph equivalence alone do not establish molecular stability, actual synthesis or an observed chemical structure.

